    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d368">

                <pb facs="0550" n="450" sameAs="frus:frus1934v01/pg450"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A14/633a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the American Delegate to the
                        General Disarmament Conference</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Wilson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-05-01T18:00:00-04:00">May 1, 1934—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>144. The Senate Committee on Foreign Relations today recommended unanimously that
                    the Senate give its advice and consent to <pb facs="0551" n="451" xml:id="pg451"
                    /> the ratification of the Arms Traffic Convention of 1925. They added the
                    reservation that the ratification should not become effective until the Treaty
                    was ratified by 13 specified powers, among which are the principal arms
                    producing nations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
